# FreeSail: the captain's orders for the passage of gate 5b (spec M5 §15, §20; package
# 33a). The starter routines (data/standing_orders/starter.orders) are the ship's
# standing book; these are the captain's for a passage Ushant to Falmouth by the
# reckoning and the noon sight alone. The log is hove hourly of itself and the noon
# sight taken of itself if the sky allows; what the captain adds is the master's work at
# the land and the lead, in the dialect the book reads for nothing.

# The departure: while the land is in sight astern, a bearing of it every glass, so that
# the account is fixed off the Stiff before the land is lost (the Regulations' master
# "to ascertain the latitude, longitude and variation of every headland he passes";
# N §1). "The land" is the nearest land, or at night the nearest light.
# Package 37d: a bearing gives a line, and the lookout's distance by estimation is laid
# down with it only when it is the better figure (a landfall on one mark after a long
# run), so the book takes a fix by cross bearings after the bearing, which stands when
# one mark only is in sight or the marks cut too fine.
standing order "bearings": every glass, if the land is in sight then take a bearing of the land; take a fix

# The Channel Soundings: at noon, when the sight is had and she is by account in the
# fifty-fathom water south of the Lizard, she is brought to and the deep-sea lead hove
# ("it is usual previously to bring-to the ship", Falconer 1780, 'Sounding'; Rennell's
# procedure, "get the latitude, then expect soundings around lat. 49° 30' N, and run in
# on the soundings", N §3). The lead wants under four knots of way on her; hove to she
# has one or two. The run since noon has no meaning before the first noon (the reading
# says so), so neither order fires on the morning's run from Ushant.
standing order "bring to for soundings": at noon then heave to
standing order "soundings": when the speed is under 3 knots and the distance run since noon is under 5 miles then heave the deep-sea lead

# Once the cast is had she fills away, and when the fill-away has left the helm the
# course for Falmouth is shaped afresh from the account as the day's work left it; the
# sounding and the filling away are events the dialect names (spec M5 §15).
standing order "fill away after the cast": at a sounding, if the distance run since noon is under 5 miles then fill away
standing order "stand on": at filled away then shape a course for 50 02 N 4 58 W

# The landfall: the first land seen gives a bearing, and the course for Falmouth is
# shaped from the reckoning (never the truth: spec §15's "shape a course for <place>").
# The land is in sight from the start (Ushant astern), so this fires at the departure
# too, which shapes the same course north by east; it fires again when the Lizard is
# raised after the land has been out of sight.
standing order "landfall": when the land is in sight then take a bearing of the land; shape a course for 50 02 N 4 58 W

# Package 37e: a course shaped is now a course made good, the master's tide allowed in
# it (spec M5 §15), and the order's own words say what the line does: from the Lizard's
# landfall the line for Falmouth "crosses the land about Black Head" and "passes the
# Manacles within a mile". Until 37e the heading for Falmouth was held and the flood set
# her a league to the eastward of that line, clear of the ledge; made good, the line is
# sailed and she struck. So the book rounds the Manacles as the merchant passage's does,
# by a point three miles east of them (50 02 N 4 58 W), and shapes for Falmouth when it
# is within a mile by the account, which the bearings and the fix hold to a few cables.
# Package 37f: and shortens sail there, to stand in for the road at dusk under her
# reefed topsails. Standing in at six knots and a half she was hailed by the pilot's
# cutter ten minutes before her anchor, and her sail, shortened at the hail as the next
# order has it, was not in before she let go: he did not board. At a little under six
# knots from the point he boards a minute after his hail.
standing order "round the Manacles": when the distance to 50 02 N 4 58 W is under 1 mile then shape a course for Falmouth; shorten sail
# Package 37f: a course shaped is worked once, for the way she has as it is shaped, and
# the master does not alter the helm after. Brought to for the noon's cast she now has
# her way taken off before "Hove to" is said, and fills away with a knot and a half on
# her where she had four: the course shaped as she filled, "steer NNW to make it good"
# against three quarters of a knot of flood, was a point too far to the westward for the
# seven knots she had ten minutes after, she raised the Lizard a league further west, and
# passed the point more than a mile wide by her own account and stood on up the Channel.
# So the course for the point is worked again every glass on the way to it, as the
# merchant passage's is for the Iroise: from the noon's cast until the point is within a
# mile, and not once she has rounded it (the point is eight miles from the town, and on
# her way to it from the southward she is nine and more).
standing order "the course for the point": every glass, if the distance run since noon exceeds 1 mile and the distance to 50 02 N 4 58 W is over 1 mile and the distance to Falmouth is over 9 miles then shape a course for 50 02 N 4 58 W

# The pilot (package 37e): the Falmouth cutter hails her as she stands in, "shorten sail
# and he will come aboard", and the boat boards a ship making under six knots over the
# ground. Until 37e she stood in N by E at five knots and three quarters and he boarded
# of himself; from the point east of the Manacles she stands in NNW with the wind freer,
# at six and a half, and he was left astern. So sail is shortened at his hail, as he asks.
# Package 37h: he boards only when he is taken at his hail, and `take the pilot` shortens
# sail as the hail asks when she is too fast for his boat; the book takes him.
standing order "the pilot boards": at the pilot's hail then take the pilot

# Running in: the hand lead every glass once the land is in sight and the run since
# noon says she is closing the coast (Master, art. XXVI: "always sounding to inform
# himself of the situation of the Ship"); she heaves to in twelve fathoms, which is the
# outer road off Pendennis (Imray 1874: the outer road in 11 to 14 fathoms). Twenty miles
# since noon is judgement: past the soundings and closing the land.
standing order "lead going": every glass, if the land is in sight and the distance run since noon exceeds 20 miles then heave the lead

# Closing the land the lead goes oftener: every ten minutes once she is within the
# thirty-five miles since noon that put her off the Manacles, so that the first bottom
# found is not already the Roads' banks (the lead, 2026-09-30: with the lead every glass
# the first cast under twenty fathoms was five and a half, and she took the ground hove to
# in it; Luce 1884 ch. XXV, the lead "kept going" when standing in). The outer road off
# Pendennis lies in eleven to fourteen fathoms (Imray 1874); she heaves to at twelve.
standing order "lead going in": every 10 minutes, if the land is in sight and the distance run since noon exceeds 35 miles then heave the lead

# At the first cast under twenty fathoms she is in the outer road, ten to fourteen
# fathoms of good ground off Pendennis (Imray 1874, p. 90: "whence a ship may go to sea
# or run into Carrick Road"), and there she comes to an anchor (package 34; spec M5
# §18): all hands, the light sails and the courses in, the helm down for the stronger
# of wind and tide, the best bower let go with the way off her, the cable veered to
# five times the depth, the sails furled (Luce 1866 ch. XXXIV, 'Having a leading wind,
# to run in and anchor', pp. 569 to 571). Before package 34 she wore here and lay to
# on the starboard tack, forereaching off the land, Falmouth Bay being a lee shore in a
# south-westerly; a ship brought up rides to it.
standing order "the outer road": when the depth is under 20 fathoms then come to an anchor
